"Aline Dyce has enrolled three students in five years who had no card, on Hallick's word."
"Dyce keeps a second ledger, hidden in the record room, of work the Institute does unpaid."
"The Institute's licence to teach is granted by the Academy, and lapses if it is ever reviewed."
"Hallick's chamber holds a bed, a stove and a wall of books he brought from Geldor."
"Hallick sleeps at the Institute more nights than at his house on Hill Road."
"The record room's oldest boxes hold sequences the Institute has taught since before the Academy."
"Fourteen claimants have been set the lens table in eleven years, and none saw the proportion."
"Sera Wyle published a partial answer in Geldor; Hallick's is further along and unpublished."
"A clean lens rule would improve every lamp, watch, lens and instrument the Empire makes."
"The Army's range tables rest on the same gap, and Hallick knows it well enough to fear it."
"Hallick will want any working she writes entered in his ledger, under his name and the Institute's."
"Nala has no paper or pen, so the working must go on Hallick's slate, in her own hand."
"Anything she writes on that slate stays in the workshop, where apprentices and Dyce can see it."
"Hallick has carried the slate to Geldor twice and shown it to no one who found the 120."
"Hallick will enter the rule in his own ledger tonight, in his own hand, under his own name."
"A lens rule lets the Institute promise any focus, and takes that work off every rival in the south."
"A rule read off a drawn curve is the part Hallick cannot price, and the part he wants most."
"Hallick would sooner have her lodge at the Institute than walk out to Dyce, the Registry or the Courier."
"Dyce keeps a second ledger of pupils taken on the master's word, and none of them had a card."
"Taking a cardless pupil on is Hallick's own risk: the licence to teach is the Academy's to withdraw."
"The apprentices will be in at quarter past seven, and one of them talks to everyone."
